00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.300
Let's take a look at
the production system.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.484
So this is a large-scale
production system.

00:07:06.484 --> 00:07:07.900
And one of the
early findings here

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.030
is that a machine
learning project is not

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.060
just like we're going to
go to machine learning.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.210
We don't do it in a vacuum.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:18.430
Instead, it exists as part of a
much larger production system.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:20.920
And when you're talking about
the recently announced 800

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.680
million monthly active
people who used Drive,

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:25.990
thousands of businesses
across all of the platforms

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:30.550
that we've just seen, this is a
large-scale production system.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.560
We have to have rigor
around the servers, all

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.330
the data processing pipelines.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:37.180
Even the UX must be very
carefully thought of.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.730
Dozens of servers
are involved, and it

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:42.100
is a Google-scale project
end to end, which means

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.915
it's hard but very exciting.

00:07:43.915 --> 00:07:46.496
It takes a lot of
infrastructure to get here.

00:07:46.496 --> 00:07:49.120
Some examples are on the right,
and I just kind of put together

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:49.852
this list.

00:07:49.852 --> 00:07:52.060
So it's the machine learning
frameworks and tool kits

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:53.020
that you would expect--

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.900
Google Brain; DistBelief, which
is our internal tool kit that

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:58.540
we use right now; increasingly
in the future, TensorFlow,

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:01.180
which is our open source
machine learning framework;

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.760
all of the data processing,
large-scale analytics that we

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:06.820
use with parallel processing
on the back via MapReduce,

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:10.240
which our core distributed
computational technology;

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.480
an experiment framework to be
able to evaluate hypotheses

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.330
to figure out whether you're
actually improving the system;

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:18.340
the ability to query
large-scale data;

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:21.160
all the stuff you need to stand
up servers and multiple data

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:21.790
centers--

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:26.020
load balancing, deployment,
APIs and the remote procedure

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:28.690
calls that you make in between
them; all of the dashboard

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.120
visualizations; and
finally, a framework

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.929
to be able to do statistics well
because doing statistics well,

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:37.099
it turns out to
be very difficult.

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:38.390
Here's an architecture diagram.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.764
We won't go too
deeply into this,

00:08:39.764 --> 00:08:41.679
but the general idea
here is that to be

00:08:41.679 --> 00:08:43.460
able to predict
something like this,

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:47.500
you'll find that there
are a lot of pieces here.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.810
In this case, there's the
offline model training.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.310
We're training up
a neural network.

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:54.100
That's an offline process
that takes one or two days.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:55.640
We'll see that here in a moment.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:58.270
And then the evaluation is
done online or in real time

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:00.520
when a client makes
a call to generate

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.220
the most probable documents
that you want to show.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:05.301
But on top of this is
all of the metrics,

00:09:05.301 --> 00:09:06.800
and the logging,
and the dashboards,

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:08.290
and the experiment framework.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:12.400
And then very critically, there
is a dedicated service and team

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.730
that is actually responsible
for aggregating and anonymizing

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:17.950
and generating the
data that is ultimately

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:21.290
used to train models and indeed
extract features that are used

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:22.540
when we're making predictions.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:25.150
That's Activity
service in the middle.

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:28.780
So it turns out the actual
science, the machine learning

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:32.230
aspect of it, is one
comparatively small part

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:34.859
of a much larger
production system.

00:09:34.859 --> 00:09:36.400
It's an exciting
result, but it means

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.680
we have to really
focus on what we

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:40.729
call the [? prodeng ?] as well.

00:09:40.729 --> 00:09:43.270
So one thing we'll do today is
learn about the actual machine

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:46.030
learning that we do here at
Google, and in particular,

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:47.290
for this.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:49.930
Machine learning in general
requires three broad things--

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:52.790
features, data, and algorithms.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.340
So features are
basically the signals

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:56.110
that you can use to
kind of separate data

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.400
and to make predictions.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:02.110
In other domains, they're
called inputs or predictors.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:04.900
Multiple features together,
these are signals extracted,

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:06.370
from this case, documents.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:09.559
Multiple features together
form a feature vector

00:10:09.559 --> 00:10:12.100
where a feature vector can have
a lot of different features--

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:16.010
in this case, tens of thousands
in this particular project.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:17.510
Some of those
features are raw data.

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:18.700
Some of them are engineered.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:20.200
They're functions
of other features.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.060
We apply certain
transformations.

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:24.850
In general, we do fairly
minimal post-processing, not too

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.380
much scaling or
anything like that.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.690
The neural networks are very
adaptive to kind of take

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:30.490
anything that you
want to throw at them.

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:33.520
And some examples of features
that we would use here

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.400
include per document
counts of the number

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:39.790
of times a document is
opened, when it was last open,

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:41.020
these kinds of things.

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:44.200
The time or the temporal
aspect turns out

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.600
to be a very predictive
and powerful feature.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:48.640
And so we encode
this as periodicity.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:51.430
And what that means is if you
open a document-- and this is

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:53.950
an example of a feature that
we found to be very compelling.

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:56.200
If you open a document
every Saturday morning,

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.140
maybe your household budget
at 8:00 AM over your coffee,

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.310
and you do that every
Saturday at 8:00 AM,

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.880
well, that is actually
very strongly predictive

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:06.820
of you probably doing that
next Saturday as well.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:08.430
Great opportunity
to save you time

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.930
so you don't have to drill down
into your folders or search.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:12.550
So if we can encode
that correctly

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.258
with a little bit of
feature engineering,

00:11:14.258 --> 00:11:16.310
we have on our hands a
very powerful feature,

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:18.980
which overall is part of the
impact of the intelligence

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:19.480
here.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.257
So we also have user models.

00:11:21.257 --> 00:11:23.590
We understand a little bit
about the user and the things

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:25.540
that they do, their
actual behaviors,

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:28.600
not conditioned or on an
aspect of any specific document

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:30.250
but about the user themselves.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.950
And those turn out to be
useful signals for the model.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.370
And then finally,
in the future--

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:36.630
and we'll nudge against
this here shortly--

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.240
but there's a lot
that we can do here

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.747
that requires a little
bit deeper understanding

00:11:40.747 --> 00:11:42.830
beyond just documents, but
for example, the user's

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:44.220
social graph.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:47.180
For example, if my boss has
been commenting on a file

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:50.570
that I'm working
on is probably more

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:54.770
impactful to me than somebody
further in my network.

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:56.540
So we want to capture
this mathematically

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.665
in terms of the statistics
and the model prediction

00:11:58.665 --> 00:12:00.680
and factor them in.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:03.650
So the features are ultimately
extracted on raw data.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:07.610
That is, in this case, produced
from an existing service

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:09.410
at Google called
Activity Service, which

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:12.500
receives and records events
on a per document level, which

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:17.450
for the scale that we operate
at at Google, is no small feat--

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:20.700
things like when the document
was created and shared, opened,

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.930
when it was commented on, all of
the things that are available,

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:26.600
metadata about the document
that we want to encode.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.240
There's also users stats,
basically the user model that

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:32.420
can be computed online,
attributes about the user,

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:34.550
and then in the future,
other deeper understanding

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:36.470
around the social graph.

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:40.000
So per document
activity and online user

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.140
modeling and processing
is the fundamental source

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:43.949
of most of this data.

00:12:43.949 --> 00:12:45.740
And at last, we come
up with the algorithm,

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:47.900
which has historically
been, as a machine learning

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.330
practitioner, one of my
favorite things to talk about,

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:52.040
except at Google, it's
just really boring

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:54.050
now because we just
say, OK, everything's

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:56.480
a deep neural network, right?

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.010
And once upon a
time, neural nets

00:12:58.010 --> 00:12:59.780
were very much not
in vogue position

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:02.030
to other things you may have
heard about, like support

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:06.740
vector machines, large ensembles
of simple linear classifiers,

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:07.670
for example.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:10.927
But with the advent of back
propagation for training,

00:13:10.927 --> 00:13:12.260
they've sort of come into vogue.

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:14.260
And as it turns out, the
only thing that matters

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:17.480
is how well the actual
algorithm does in practice.

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:19.640
And what we find is
that neural networks

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:22.250
are extremely
capable in this space

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.620
for a large variety of
problems-- searching,

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:28.010
ranking, prediction, image
classification, for example.

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:31.520
And they are very robust to
large numbers of features,

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:33.530
like in the tens of
thousands of features.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.620
If you did that for like
a support vector machine

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:40.400
where the training complexity
is a function of the number

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.590
of features, well, the model
might not ever converge

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:44.620
and you'd have not
effective classifier.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:47.060
So neural nets kind
of take dimensionality

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.130
out of the question,
which is very exciting.

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:51.147
And internally,
we use DistBelief,

00:13:51.147 --> 00:13:53.480
which is described in the
literature internal to Google.

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:55.580
But soon, we'll be
moving to TensorFlow,

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:57.530
which is the open
source machine learning

00:13:57.530 --> 00:13:59.000
tool kit that's very powerful.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:13:59.660
It's great.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:02.090
It means that people
outside of Google

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:03.800
can use the exact
same technologies

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:06.350
that we use for
prediction here, including

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:08.930
for our searching and
ranking tasks across Google.

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:13.340
So neural networks
can be wide or deep.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:14.804
Ours are not particularly deep.

00:14:14.804 --> 00:14:16.220
I think there's
five hidden layers

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:20.520
between the inputs for each
feature, and then the outputs.

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:25.370
And I think on the order of
80 nodes per hidden layer.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:28.760
So it's a fairly wide,
not super deep neural net,

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.100
which is not at all uncommon.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:34.640
OK, this is a fairly technical
talk, and you wanted details.

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.950
This is a quick slide to
talk about specific details

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:38.240
that we actually use.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:41.390
As we've seen, this is modeled
as a neural net trained

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:44.150
with what we do typically
now with conventional back

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:46.130
propagation.

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:49.220
And training uses asynchronous
stochastic gradient descent

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:51.920
in particular, as described
in the literature.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.570
We use 40,000
features right now.

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:54.740
That's a lot of features.

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.220
We probably don't
need that many.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.010
We suspect we can
do just as well

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:01.105
with our predictions for Quick
Access with fewer features.

00:15:01.105 --> 00:15:02.480
Feature selection
is a little bit

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.720
difficult for a neural network,
as is interpreting the model,

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:07.430
but we're going to
work towards that.

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:09.950
This is set up as ultimately
a two-class classification

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:10.850
problem.

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:12.980
The probability
that you're going

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:16.310
to open this document right
now or not that, right,

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:17.720
and on a continuous
output, which

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:22.070
is the output of the neural net,
and separate out the boundary

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:23.490
and rank as desired.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:25.670
And so you need positive
training examples of things

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:28.295
that the user has opened in the
past and then negative training

00:15:28.295 --> 00:15:30.410
examples of things that
the user has not opened.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:33.530
So that forms the basis
of our classification.

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:35.987
Training is a very large task.

00:15:35.987 --> 00:15:37.070
We have a lot of features.

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:40.310
It may take one or two
days for the actual model--

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:42.050
and we're generating one model--

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:43.860
to evolve and to converge.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:46.700
And there's lots of
cross-validation and creating

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:48.800
multiple replicas.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.800
And it's a very
computationally intensive task

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:53.380
distributed across,
I think, a couple

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:56.240
of hundred individual
processors,

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:57.160
workers in this case.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:59.960
So this is a fairly
large endeavor.

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:01.820
But of course, we do
all of that work, which

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:03.140
is basically pre-computation.

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:03.770
Why?

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:06.500
So we can evaluate
quickly in real time.

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:09.280
When a user opens up and
when we open up Drive,

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:10.250
we see Quick Access.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:13.250
That is a real-time request to
get the most probable documents

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:16.269
for the user in that moment
based on the state of all

00:16:16.269 --> 00:16:17.060
of their documents.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:19.476
And that means we're extracting
the features in real time.

00:16:19.476 --> 00:16:21.680
And we can do that on the
order of 100 milliseconds

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:22.539
in the common case.

00:16:22.539 --> 00:16:24.080
So we do all that
work training to be

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:28.220
able to come up with a very
quickly evaluated model, which

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:30.780
is one of the benefits of
a neural network model.

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:34.200
So good.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:35.959
So taking a step back
from the details,

00:16:35.959 --> 00:16:37.750
I wanted to get a little
bit philosophical.

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:39.370
I've been thinking
about this a lot.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:41.950
One of my favorite
things about Google

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.320
is this increasing machine
learning-aware culture

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:45.580
that we have here.

00:16:45.580 --> 00:16:47.080
And now when I first
started, it was

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:49.330
we'd use machine learning
in like Search, for example,

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:51.310
but it did not have
this cultural presence

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:53.035
that it does now.

00:16:53.035 --> 00:16:53.910
Times were different.

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:55.960
And It's what I
went to school for.

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:58.210
So to see it kind of take
hold is very exciting.

00:16:58.210 --> 00:17:00.610
But it's not just individual
engineering competencies.

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:03.054
It's everything that it takes
to land a feature like this

00:17:03.054 --> 00:17:03.970
that we have a Google.

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:07.180
So the concept of having
privacy front and center,

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:09.819
and thinking about that, and
talking candidly about it,

00:17:09.819 --> 00:17:13.180
and having privacy experts who
are aware of common concerns

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:14.200
that we could have--

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:16.359
a very privacy-centric
culture is

00:17:16.359 --> 00:17:19.180
critical to doing
this project properly.

00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:21.079
Cloud expertise, this
is a cloud project.

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:23.750
We're not operating on
local data on a filer.

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.829
So we need to be very good about
processing data in the cloud

00:17:26.829 --> 00:17:28.780
and doing that in
a thoughtful way.

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:30.430
And it takes leadership
buy-in, right?

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:32.870
This is not just an
individual engineer's project.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:34.900
This is a large endeavor,
the whole concept

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:37.900
of intelligence features
and assisting the user.

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:40.930
Very important to have
leadership buy-in, all

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:43.420
the way down from team leads
to engineering managers

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:46.690
to executives, right, to
kind of transform the culture

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:48.347
and to support a
project like this,

00:17:48.347 --> 00:17:49.930
because it's fairly
expensive in terms

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:51.910
of how many people
need to support it

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:53.662
and the research
that we've done.

00:17:53.662 --> 00:17:55.870
Of course, we've seen all
the system's infrastructure

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:57.040
that's required.

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:59.770
Product managers need to
be bought into the value

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:01.210
proposition to
the users and need

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:03.970
to be shown that we're
actually helping users.

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:06.074
There needs to be an
enterprise focus increasingly

00:18:06.074 --> 00:18:07.990
because that's where we
see most of the impact

00:18:07.990 --> 00:18:09.430
from features like this.

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:11.950
We need to do the UI
and the UX very well

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:13.690
because we can do
harm here, right,

00:18:13.690 --> 00:18:15.400
if we surface a
great feature that

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:17.170
doesn't have a compelling UX.

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:19.240
We'll see some screenshots
of this here shortly.

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:20.740
And just in general,
broader culture

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:23.020
around learning and
intelligence, and having

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:24.880
classes that people
can take just because,

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:28.180
and learning about statistics
and being able to increasingly

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:30.730
just think, how can we do
this kind of naive thing maybe

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:32.060
a little more intelligently?

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:34.772
So I've really come to
appreciate the increasing

00:18:34.772 --> 00:18:36.230
machine learning
culture at Google,

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:37.690
and I have seen
that it is really

00:18:37.690 --> 00:18:42.320
required to be able to pull
off a feature like this.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:43.820
So we've seen how
we can use machine

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:45.042
learning to generate models.

00:18:45.042 --> 00:18:47.000
One of the most important
things that we can do

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.530
is measure our success.

00:18:48.530 --> 00:18:51.450
Measurement is critical
for any project like this.

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:53.450
My dad used to tell me,
measure twice, cut once.

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:56.810
I didn't always obey that,
but I definitely do now.

00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:59.000
Our approach is
basically threefold.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:01.606
We define rigorous metrics
that are meaningful.

00:19:01.606 --> 00:19:03.230
These are numbers
that you can actually

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:04.970
use, optimize a model to.

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:07.070
It's a very mathematical
thing, right--

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:09.510
a loss function or an
optimization function.

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:12.410
So we define these
summary metrics ultimately

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:15.982
to capture what we think the
user cares about, right--

00:19:15.982 --> 00:19:17.690
how often we're
predicting the right sort

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:19.550
of thing, how often
they're engaging

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:21.080
with Quick Access in general.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.850
They have to be
carefully defined.

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:25.070
And then you can't
just report a number.

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:27.650
You need to do proper
statistics around them.

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:32.570
And if you're comparing
different sorts of approaches

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:34.250
in an experimental
fashion, you need

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:36.650
to be able to compute
confidence intervals on the test

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:38.899
group versus the control
group to figure out if you've

00:19:38.899 --> 00:19:39.999
actually helped or hurt.

00:19:39.999 --> 00:19:41.540
And then finally,
it's very important

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:43.280
to have a sensible baseline.

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:45.950
In this case, we're going to
predict your most recently

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:51.170
used documents as a baseline,
things you most recently opened

00:19:51.170 --> 00:19:51.722
or edited.

00:19:51.722 --> 00:19:53.180
And we want to do
better than that.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:55.013
If we're not doing
better than our baseline,

00:19:55.013 --> 00:19:58.670
we're not really doing an
intelligent, impactful thing.

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:00.940
Specific metrics that we
use, that we care about--

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:02.620
and they all capture
different things.

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:06.904
First, the astute viewer will
note that all are going upwards

00:20:06.904 --> 00:20:07.820
and towards the right.

00:20:07.820 --> 00:20:10.070
That's a very good thing.

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:11.750
I wouldn't call them
fully hockey stick,

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.940
but they're trending in
the correct direction.

00:20:13.940 --> 00:20:15.470
That's not an accident.

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:19.310
We want to rigorously improve
this continually, and only

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:21.710
through doing that will
we land at a useful thing.

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:23.480
Examples of the three
metrics we use--

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:27.490
hit rate measures the actual
percentage of the time

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:29.330
that Quick Access
was correctly used

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:31.100
to predict the doc
that the user wants--

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.360
in other words, the
percentage of the time

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:34.359
that the user found their
document through Quick

00:20:34.359 --> 00:20:37.820
Access, which is subtly
different than accuracy, which

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:39.440
is more the
percentage of the time

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:42.110
that Quick Access predicted
the document that the user

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:44.120
ultimately did open.

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:45.099
See the nuance there?

00:20:45.099 --> 00:20:46.640
And then finally,
click-through rate,

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:48.710
which is more a measure
of engagement with the UI

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:49.612
feature itself.

00:20:49.612 --> 00:20:51.320
What percentage of
the time are the users

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:54.830
actually using Quick Access
when Quick Access is available?

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:56.900
These don't paint the
identical picture.

00:20:56.900 --> 00:21:01.190
It is possible to optimize for
one of these but not the other.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:02.930
And now we have
a problem, right?

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.350
So we consider all three
of these metrics together

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:07.250
and we want to see
them improve together

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:10.250
having seen firsthand that it's
possible to improve one and not

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:11.900
the other.

00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:14.000
Similarly, we have
production metrics.

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:15.350
There's a lot of dependencies.

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:16.790
This is not a science project.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:19.850
We are constantly monitoring
the health of what

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:21.740
we consider to be a service.

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:24.470
The three key things here
are QPS, Queries Per Second;

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:25.160
latency--

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:26.720
in other words, how
fast are we doing

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:28.460
this where fast is a
very important thing

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.200
to do things in--

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:31.520
as well as error rate.

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:36.080
We want this to be a reliable,
error-free experience.

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:38.360
Examples of what this looks
like, and we track them.

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:39.500
QPS is on the left.

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:41.750
You can see kind of
our natural cycle.

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:42.870
We do have a daily cycle.

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:47.187
There is peak traffic at a
specific time during the day.

00:21:47.187 --> 00:21:48.770
The challenge of any
production system

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:51.350
is coping with peak load,
where in general, we

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:53.562
try and smooth things out
to the extent possible.

00:21:53.562 --> 00:21:54.770
We've done some load testing.

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:56.728
And of course, there's
organic platform growth.

00:21:56.728 --> 00:21:58.400
We're rolling out
to other platforms.

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:00.650
And additional users are
coming to those platforms,

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:03.200
and that will be
reflected in that.

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:05.600
Similarly, we always
compare QPS with latency

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:07.520
because there is a
relationship here,

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.204
that the more you throw at
your servers, the more queries,

00:22:10.204 --> 00:22:12.620
on average, the slower things
are going to get, especially

00:22:12.620 --> 00:22:16.730
if you have to queue requests or
there's other capacity issues.

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:18.770
We've made some major
strides with our latency.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:21.455
And we look at things like
latency in the average case,

00:22:21.455 --> 00:22:23.330
but how about latency
like in the worst case?

00:22:23.330 --> 00:22:25.490
Maybe you have a
particularly large corpus

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:26.700
or a large number of files.

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:28.820
So we never look at these
numbers in isolation.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:31.700
We talk about things like
median latency and latency

00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:34.200
at the 95th percentile,
for example.

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:36.440
So we've done some
optimizations here--

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:37.640
launch and iterate, right?

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:39.950
Always optimizing,
finding the bottleneck.

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:41.960
Parallelization solves
a lot of problems

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:44.134
as well as just throwing
more hardware at things.

00:22:44.134 --> 00:22:46.550
If you're under provisions and
slow, well, maybe add a few

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:48.890
more tasks or instances
of the server that

00:22:48.890 --> 00:22:51.630
can receive these requests.

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:55.530
Pivoting to dashboards, this
is one of my favorite aspects

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:56.452
about this project.

00:22:56.452 --> 00:22:58.410
It would be very easy to
do something like this

00:22:58.410 --> 00:23:00.930
and have ad hoc
analyses on [? one ?]

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:03.360
running one-off queries
by one or two people.

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:05.996
But instead, we've
institutionalized the things

00:23:05.996 --> 00:23:07.620
that we care about,
and we've done that

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:09.828
through a dashboard, which
is critical infrastructure

00:23:09.828 --> 00:23:11.280
in its own right.

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:13.364
They show real-time
results of the system.

00:23:13.364 --> 00:23:15.030
They show the results
of the experiments

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:16.530
that we're trying to do.

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:18.450
Here, we've scrubbed the numbers
because they're sensitive,

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:20.010
but you can see our
three key metrics

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:23.250
are there, but also the
confidence intervals and then

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:25.170
the comparison
with our baseline.

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:28.560
So it helps us to kind of
get cultural knowledge around

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:29.504
how well we're doing.

00:23:29.504 --> 00:23:31.170
And of course, they're
very transparent.

00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:32.610
Everything is transparent here.

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:34.320
And critically,
this is always based

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:37.680
on heavily aggregated,
anonymized summary statistics

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:38.340
data--

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:40.692
very critical for dashboards.

00:23:40.692 --> 00:23:42.150
Other examples from
the dashboards,

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:44.850
we have our historical
primary metrics over time,

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:48.360
both in graph time series format
and in tabular format, as well

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:50.520
as how much time
we've saved the user.

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:51.800
That's very important to us.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.000
And we can actually chart
that as kind of an area

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:56.460
under the curve sort of a thing.

00:23:56.460 --> 00:24:00.150
Positional statistics count,
this is an interesting finding.

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:03.360
We find that the majority of
impact that comes from this

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:06.390
is going to be in the
first n predictions, right?

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.660
And so if all of the
benefit of your feature

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:11.307
comes from predicting
just three things

00:24:11.307 --> 00:24:13.140
and you're getting it
right so much that you

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:15.450
don't need to predict
the other things, well--

00:24:15.450 --> 00:24:18.330
the other seven things or
have an extended carousel,

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:21.120
you can imagine that that's
important for UI and UX.

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:23.340
In particular,
maybe our carousel

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:25.410
doesn't need to
have 10 items in it

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:28.980
if we're always or usually
right at the first three or five

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:29.700
things.

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:32.460
So these dashboards,
this data feeds back

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:36.370
into a number of decisions.

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:38.860
Three challenges that
were particularly--

00:24:38.860 --> 00:24:41.860
and by "challenging," I don't
mean unpleasant or difficult.

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:44.910
I mean intriguing and
very engaging, right?

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:49.360
It's the challenging stuff
that makes for a an exciting

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:50.470
engineering day.

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:53.390
Privacy is perhaps our
most critical challenge,

00:24:53.390 --> 00:24:55.330
but it's also built
into our culture.

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:57.940
And so it's this wonderful
dialogue that we continually

00:24:57.940 --> 00:25:01.420
have about every time we
code or do a design review,

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:03.790
we're constantly poking
holes in things to say,

00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:06.334
could that data leak, or do we
need to aggregate that data?

00:25:06.334 --> 00:25:07.250
And the answer is yes.

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:09.880
Do we need to have you
anonymize this data?

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:12.470
We're very careful with log
sources, these sorts of things.

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:14.470
We think about this
cross-functionally, not just

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:17.230
engineers, not just leaders,
but product managers and program

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:17.980
managers.

00:25:17.980 --> 00:25:19.360
It's always on our mind.

00:25:19.360 --> 00:25:20.950
And importantly,
there are experts

00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:24.040
who see these things all
day everyday and understand

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:25.870
the common modes
or areas of concern

00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:27.730
that we might have
around privacy,

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:29.900
both in different
usage modalities--

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:33.190
for example, enterprise
versus consumer users outside

00:25:33.190 --> 00:25:34.690
of the enterprise.

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:36.890
It's a passion, and
it's almost an--

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:39.472
it is an interesting
problem in its own right.

00:25:39.472 --> 00:25:41.680
We understand that we have
very important constraints

00:25:41.680 --> 00:25:42.490
that we have on the data.

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:45.370
We need to do right by the user,
and we need to be very careful.

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:49.000
And so how can we
make experiments

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:51.460
around hypotheses based
on aggregated data?

00:25:51.460 --> 00:25:53.470
How can we still
build a useful system

00:25:53.470 --> 00:25:56.620
with these very careful
controls in place?

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:58.520
It's a science problem
in its own right.

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:00.380
So a couple of specific things.

00:26:00.380 --> 00:26:03.040
We don't allow raw
access on the engineers

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:04.540
to any kind of user data.

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:06.290
We don't allow user tracking.

00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:09.040
Instead, we work with anonymized
data and aggregated summary

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:09.940
statistics.

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:12.730
And as noted, enterprise
data is specifically not

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:16.200
available for use in most cases.

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:19.110
Another challenge is the
challenge around data quality.

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:21.390
So we have an existing
server that was first built

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:23.980
to generate document data--

00:26:23.980 --> 00:26:25.740
and this is a fairly
old screenshot--

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:28.260
but to generate activity
data for a given

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:30.930
document in the Drive
clients, in the UIs.

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:34.704
And that is a very different
task than generating data

00:26:34.704 --> 00:26:36.870
that's going to be used by
a machine learning system

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:38.670
for a neural network
that's saying,

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:40.484
feed me features all the time.

00:26:40.484 --> 00:26:42.150
So that's actually
one of our challenges

00:26:42.150 --> 00:26:45.900
is it's very fortunate that
we have this infrastructure,

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.069
but it's a new use case
for an existing service.

00:26:48.069 --> 00:26:49.860
That means there might
be holes in the data

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:53.050
or that we may need to
process some of the data more.

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:55.200
Not all the things
that we need are there.

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:56.730
And of course,
ultimately, we just

00:26:56.730 --> 00:27:00.210
hooked up a very
large-scale system

00:27:00.210 --> 00:27:02.610
to an existing server
that needs to be

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:05.010
very carefully provisioned
for that around rollout.

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:07.140
So data quality,
availability, server

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:10.050
load is one of these kind of
classic production engineering

00:27:10.050 --> 00:27:11.670
challenges that we have.

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:14.760
And then finally, pivoting
towards UI and UX,

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:18.600
it is not easy to
create a useful UI here.

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:22.020
And part of the challenge is
is that as your predictions get

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:24.810
better and better, you may
be able to finally land

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:25.800
a different UI.

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:28.380
You may be able to have
a more prominent UI.

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:31.500
Maybe it's shown all the time
only once your predictions

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:32.580
get good enough.

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:34.020
At first, we
weren't sure, right?

00:27:34.020 --> 00:27:37.410
And we would pop up, as you
see on the left, a modal dialog

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:41.800
only when our predictions were
above a very high confidence,

00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:43.560
well, because they
weren't always.

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:46.240
We hadn't launched and we
hadn't iterated enough.

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.270
But over time, we
got we got better.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:52.890
And we are in an era of where
we have user UX design and UX

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:53.730
research.

00:27:53.730 --> 00:27:58.710
And just like we run
experiments on our hypotheses

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:01.750
for ideas around features,
for our intelligence,

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:05.790
so too do we do research
around how a given UI looks.

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:08.700
So we have a very strong
culture on prototype

00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:11.940
and doing user
studies and iterating.

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:13.667
And at last, we
ended up on the UI

00:28:13.667 --> 00:28:15.750
that you can see here today
on your mobile device,

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:19.322
on iOS or Android, which
is the one on the right.

00:28:19.322 --> 00:28:21.780
And even then, we're iterating,
as you'll see in a few more

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:23.850
screenshots, to where--

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:26.610
we'll talk about that-- to a
more different design showing

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:29.400
larger thumbnails.

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:32.700
Experiments are a
critical part of the way

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:36.830
that we are able to
improve the overall system.

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:40.310
In general, it is very easy
to say, hey, you have an idea.

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:42.930
Let's make a new feature
and put it out there.

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:45.130
And you have no way to
know if it helped it.

00:28:45.130 --> 00:28:47.940
Certainly, you could
drum up a use case

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:49.620
that shows, hey, look at this.

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:52.080
I did this thing and now this
new thing is being predicted,

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:52.788
and it's perfect.

00:28:52.788 --> 00:28:54.840
But maybe you made it
worse for everybody.

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:57.180
And in fact, you probably did
because improving a system

00:28:57.180 --> 00:28:58.620
like this is not
a trivial thing.

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:02.790
And features that you think may
be useful for you may not be.

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:06.360
And yet, this kind of
creative hypothesis generation

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:09.240
is at the center of how we can
potentially improve things.

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:10.590
This is science, basically.

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:12.100
It's a science experiment.

00:29:12.100 --> 00:29:17.190
So we have a very rigorous
kind of cultural use

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:18.930
of the experiment
framework, which

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:21.570
is part of our common
infrastructure at Google.

00:29:21.570 --> 00:29:23.430
So we can rigorously
test the hypothesis

00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:27.450
and figure out whether we're
actually improving or hurting.

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:29.820
And over time, the
model accuracy improves

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:32.550
and we hope that user
adoption does as well.

00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:36.870
A few case studies here
to look at how this works.

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:41.460
We encode how recently an
item is opened or edited,

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:43.950
and we're trying to figure out
how best to represent that.

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:45.630
In other words,
we're actually trying

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:50.220
to figure out how to engineer
the raw data into a feature

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:54.480
that the neural network
will most effectively be

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:56.460
able to use,
optimizing ultimately

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:58.170
for your summary metrics.

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:02.010
So the hypothesis here is that
opening or editing something

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:04.880
within one or five
or 10 minutes,

00:30:04.880 --> 00:30:08.030
basically showing a histogram
of counts of the number of times

00:30:08.030 --> 00:30:10.290
it was done there,
could be useful,

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.040
but there's so many
free parameters here--

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:13.870
basically, the bucket
size of the histogram,

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:15.494
which is a very common
way to represent

00:30:15.494 --> 00:30:17.560
feature vectors for machine
learning algorithms.

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:19.890
So we run an experiment,
right, multiple experiments

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:22.410
to figure out, what
will the model best

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:24.674
be able to predict with?

00:30:24.674 --> 00:30:27.090
And ultimately, we found that
encoding a feature in bucket

00:30:27.090 --> 00:30:30.330
size that captures the fact
that a file has been acted on

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:32.970
at one in five minute
intervals was impactful.

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:34.980
And the result is
we correctly chose

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:36.900
the histogram of
bucketing size to be

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.720
able to give our model
the effective feature

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:42.540
representation to be able
to make good predictions.

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:44.310
You can see how
nuanced this is, right?

00:30:44.310 --> 00:30:46.680
We wouldn't be able to
just do this arbitrarily,

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:49.260
and it was only through
statistically significant

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:53.520
experiments that we can find
that, hey, we did well here.

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:56.790
Another case study is
using Calendar data.

00:30:56.790 --> 00:30:59.049
So there's a magic moment
here, which is basically,

00:30:59.049 --> 00:31:00.840
you have a meeting
coming up and somebody's

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:03.526
attached an attachment
to the meeting, something

00:31:03.526 --> 00:31:05.400
that you need to go over
ahead of the meeting

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:06.584
or during the meeting.

00:31:06.584 --> 00:31:08.750
So you don't want to be
chatting links to the thing.

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.950
Everybody needs to have
the agenda pulled up

00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:14.040
on their document or
whatever on their laptop.

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:15.510
And the idea is
that, hey, we think

00:31:15.510 --> 00:31:18.004
we can do a cool
thing here, but we

00:31:18.004 --> 00:31:20.670
have a little bit of humility to
know that maybe it's not always

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:21.336
the right thing.

00:31:21.336 --> 00:31:26.940
And we also need to understand
we have to figure out

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:28.152
when to show this, right?

00:31:28.152 --> 00:31:29.610
So if I show you
a document that is

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:32.035
going to be at a meeting
two weeks from now,

00:31:32.035 --> 00:31:33.410
that's not going
to be impactful.

00:31:33.410 --> 00:31:36.307
That might actually hurt
our overall statistics,

00:31:36.307 --> 00:31:37.140
the overall metrics.

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:39.030
Maybe we could have
predicted something

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:41.200
better in that first
slot, for example.

00:31:41.200 --> 00:31:42.990
So we're trying
to optimize here.

00:31:42.990 --> 00:31:44.100
So what was our finding?

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:47.190
We were able to find values
for when to show a calendar

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:51.360
attachment in the first
slot that overall had

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:53.370
an improvement to
our metrics and that

00:31:53.370 --> 00:31:56.490
had an acceptable click-through
rate for that prediction.

00:31:56.490 --> 00:32:01.040
And the hope is to be able to
do a demo of that here now.

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:03.660
Ah, demo time, OK.

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:05.910
So my friends always ask
me, why do you do live demos

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:06.701
in talks like this?

00:32:06.701 --> 00:32:08.640
It's because I like
to live dangerously,

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:12.360
said with an English accent
that I'm not able to do.

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:14.790
And then my boss, who's
in the room, always ask,

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:16.479
what could possibly go wrong?

00:32:16.479 --> 00:32:18.270
And that's just something
that we say here.

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:21.900
So nonetheless, we're going to
do a demo today to kind of show

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.390
what Quick Access looks like
on web, which is, again,

00:32:24.390 --> 00:32:26.070
rolling out in the
next couple of weeks,

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:27.736
and also look at what
Quick Access looks

00:32:27.736 --> 00:32:28.830
like for Team Drives.

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:31.810
And we'll use the calendar
attachment as our approach.

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:35.264
So it looks like we've
got the demo up there.

00:32:35.264 --> 00:32:36.930
So in this demo, there
are two accounts.

00:32:36.930 --> 00:32:40.860
There is somebody,
if I move over here,

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:42.840
in the Ink 42, which
is the test domain.

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:45.630
It's a domain that we have here
at Next, which is basically

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.020
modeling an enterprise.

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:49.380
And you can see I have Quick
Access predictions here.

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.120
I will Shift-Reload
and see what we get.

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:54.537
People are using this,
so the predictions are--

00:32:54.537 --> 00:32:56.370
so somebody just created
some meeting notes.

00:32:56.370 --> 00:32:58.140
Great, so that's showing up.

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:00.660
So there is an
existing appointment

00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:04.980
on this person's calendar,
a presentation on ML.

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:06.600
That's right here.

00:33:06.600 --> 00:33:12.060
And that appointment is shared
with this second account, which

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:13.450
is in this window right here.

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:15.300
And so the idea is that
we're going to come here,

00:33:15.300 --> 00:33:17.424
and this is the user that's
not in that enterprise.

00:33:17.424 --> 00:33:19.770
This is actually
a personal user.

00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:24.190
And they will make an attachment
to the calendar appointment

00:33:24.190 --> 00:33:26.070
shared between the two users.

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:28.922
In this case, I'll click
on this, that you're

00:33:28.922 --> 00:33:29.880
invited to the reunion.

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:32.780
Maybe they've got a little
family reunion thing going on.

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:33.530
Save it.

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:36.030
We'll send out updates.

00:33:36.030 --> 00:33:38.970
So that's going to write and
propagate into all the systems.

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:41.680
So if I come back into
Drive for this user

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:44.250
now that was in the domain
that has a shared calendar

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:47.730
appointment that just had
an attachment added to it,

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:49.680
and they happened to
go to their Drive,

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:53.250
the expectation is is that they
will see that show up there.

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:54.840
There it is, yeah.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:55.830
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:55.830 --> 00:33:57.240
OK.

00:33:57.240 --> 00:34:00.319
Oh, you all.

00:34:00.319 --> 00:34:01.860
All right, I'll keep
doing live demos

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:03.526
as long as they keep
working every time.

00:34:03.526 --> 00:34:04.870
OK, right.

00:34:04.870 --> 00:34:05.702
So that's great.

00:34:05.702 --> 00:34:06.660
There are other things.

00:34:06.660 --> 00:34:07.410
I could remove it.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:10.560
It would show up if I
removed access for this user.

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:12.449
Everything is live
and connected.

00:34:12.449 --> 00:34:17.190
So if I removed access, it would
remove itself automatically

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:18.215
from that list.

00:34:18.215 --> 00:34:19.590
While we're here,
we can drill in

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:21.360
and take a look at
what Quick Access looks

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:22.860
like for Team Drives.

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:24.719
There's a sales team
test Team Drive here.

00:34:24.719 --> 00:34:28.440
You can see that we actually
can make Quick Access document

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.540
predictions within the
context of a Team Drive--

00:34:30.540 --> 00:34:31.949
very enterprise-focused.

00:34:31.949 --> 00:34:33.929
And that's rolling out
in the next few weeks.

00:34:33.929 --> 00:34:36.960
Of course, Team Drives
just launched today.

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:40.039
We are finding early results
that show very compelling data

00:34:40.039 --> 00:34:41.580
that these predictions
are especially

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:43.949
useful in the context
of Team Drives.

00:34:43.949 --> 00:34:47.429
The problem is it seems very
applicable to machine learning.

00:34:47.429 --> 00:34:50.489
So we're able to have pretty
high results and statistics

00:34:50.489 --> 00:34:51.750
on our predictions here.

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:54.389
So that's super exciting,
and also very motivating

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:57.450
to continue to iterate here.

00:34:57.450 --> 00:34:58.872
So that's it for the demo.

00:34:58.872 --> 00:35:01.080
We have a few more slides
to kind of wrap up and talk

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:03.389
about future direction
that we can switch back

00:35:03.389 --> 00:35:04.680
to here when you have a chance.

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:05.580
Thank you.

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:08.940
Future directions-- Quick
Access is only the beginning.

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:10.740
And as we've had
success with this,

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:14.860
it's very encouraging to
have these early successes,

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:17.356
but we have a lot more
that we want to do.

00:35:17.356 --> 00:35:19.980
So here's an idea of what we can
do, both with Quick Access but

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:20.850
and beyond.

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:23.670
Of course, at Google, we have
a saying "Fast everybody's

00:35:23.670 --> 00:35:25.630
favorite feature."

00:35:25.630 --> 00:35:26.910
You have to be fast.

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:28.020
It's Quick Access.

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:29.970
You don't want to wait
two or three seconds

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:31.219
for your documents to show up.

00:35:31.219 --> 00:35:33.990
You want to wait 200 or 300
milliseconds for your documents

00:35:33.990 --> 00:35:34.930
to show up.

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:36.630
So there are things
that we can do here.

00:35:36.630 --> 00:35:39.390
We can have a cache where
we maintain the predictions.

00:35:39.390 --> 00:35:42.000
And then when the predictions
might change from the back ends

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:43.950
because the underlying
data changed,

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:47.610
we can push basically a
notification in the cache that

00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:48.990
says, you need a refresh.

00:35:48.990 --> 00:35:51.120
We'll do the pulling
offline, and then we

00:35:51.120 --> 00:35:55.410
can answer this in tens of
milliseconds, for example.

00:35:55.410 --> 00:35:57.990
Caching, of course,
known to be the solution

00:35:57.990 --> 00:36:00.350
to all known computer
science problems, but also

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:04.090
the cause of all known
bugs in production systems.

00:36:04.090 --> 00:36:06.330
So we have to be careful
with our caching.

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:09.521
And we'll probably have an
experiment with the cache

00:36:09.521 --> 00:36:10.020
on or off.

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:13.590
The hope is is that faster
results will improve accuracy,

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:14.880
but we don't know that.

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:16.500
We certainly hope it does.

00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:18.390
Also, client/server
protocol optimizations,

00:36:18.390 --> 00:36:20.610
always leaning out
the message size,

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:22.950
back and forth, leaning
out the number of server

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:25.650
calls that it takes to
actually get this data.

00:36:25.650 --> 00:36:28.240
We want increased
quality as well.

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:31.020
In general, we can make small
tweaks to the model parameters

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:32.790
and run our
experiments, but we feel

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:35.160
right now that the
majority of the larger

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:37.770
increases within the context
of this specific Quick Access

00:36:37.770 --> 00:36:42.010
feature would come from
using new signals, signals

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:44.260
from across G Suite--
so for example,

00:36:44.260 --> 00:36:47.400
the social graph, weighting
things by people who

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:50.280
are in your network, your
professional network, closer,

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:53.940
weighting those more heavily;
and then also understanding

00:36:53.940 --> 00:36:56.310
maybe even the
organization hierarchy.

00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:57.780
And then there's
also the concept

00:36:57.780 --> 00:36:59.850
of topical information
about a document,

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:02.700
wherein you're probably,
in a general basis,

00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:06.270
more interested in documents
that are about things

00:37:06.270 --> 00:37:08.290
that you have previously
read, in general.

00:37:08.290 --> 00:37:10.470
So we can extract a
topic model and then

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:12.160
do computations as well.

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:15.360
So new signals, we
hypothesize, will greatly

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:18.900
increase the quality of the
Quick Access predictions

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:21.315
even further beyond the
baseline than it is now.

00:37:21.315 --> 00:37:23.190
Of course, this is an
infrastructure project.

00:37:23.190 --> 00:37:25.800
We are always having
infrastructure efforts.

00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:27.000
We're improving our servers.

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:28.260
We're improving reliability.

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:29.760
We're adding capacity.

00:37:29.760 --> 00:37:31.770
We're migrating, in this
case, to TensorFlow,

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:35.550
which is Google's open source,
new generation machine learning

00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:36.325
tool kit.

00:37:36.325 --> 00:37:37.950
And then finally,
new features-- and by

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:40.616
"features," I don't mean machine
learning features in the sense.

00:37:40.616 --> 00:37:42.160
I mean user experiences.

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:44.280
So we've just launched
Team Drives today.

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:46.200
It represents a fairly
substantial one-

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:48.324
to two-year-long effort.

00:37:48.324 --> 00:37:49.740
There's a lot of
opportunity here.

00:37:49.740 --> 00:37:52.500
We think that there's so much
value around intelligence

00:37:52.500 --> 00:37:55.680
and assistive UIs in the
enterprise space, especially

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:57.472
in the Team space,
that we've already

00:37:57.472 --> 00:37:59.430
started envisioning a
lot of impactful features

00:37:59.430 --> 00:38:02.700
here beyond just things
like document prediction.

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:04.860
Deeper understanding and
assistance with content

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:06.370
and file organization.

00:38:06.370 --> 00:38:08.700
Some people have a very
carefully curated file

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:11.302
hierarchy, a folder
hierarchy, but not everybody.

00:38:11.302 --> 00:38:13.260
In fact, it's not even
necessarily that common.

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:16.101
And curating data takes a
lot of our brain cycles.

00:38:16.101 --> 00:38:18.600
We feel that we can probably
automate this task pretty well,

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:20.970
but that's just an example
of another feature that's

00:38:20.970 --> 00:38:22.830
somewhat different
than Quick Access

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:26.400
and yet would have the exact
same kind of approach, right,

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:28.530
around the production
infrastructure,

00:38:28.530 --> 00:38:31.560
the feature generation, the
use of a neural net perhaps,

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:33.870
and then the dashboards
and measurement rigor.

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:36.870
And then finally, if you've
seen Quick Access here,

00:38:36.870 --> 00:38:39.600
you've seen that the little
carousel was kind of placed

00:38:39.600 --> 00:38:41.100
in at the top of the UI.

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:43.650
The predictions are good enough
and we're saving enough time

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:45.180
that we feel like
we're able to give

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:51.780
a fairly high, prominent UI
experience with the prediction

00:38:51.780 --> 00:38:53.250
carousel.

00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:56.250
However, we're starting to
envision other things here

00:38:56.250 --> 00:38:58.200
around people in
teams that are harder

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:00.390
to fit into existing UIs.

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:02.820
At some point, we've kind
of shoehorned everything in.

00:39:02.820 --> 00:39:05.940
So we're starting to imagine a
completely different experience

00:39:05.940 --> 00:39:10.530
that kind of moves us past
a raw listing of files

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:13.290
and more towards kind of a
more holistic intelligence

00:39:13.290 --> 00:39:15.090
and assistive experience.

00:39:15.090 --> 00:39:18.090
So that's coming soon.

00:39:18.090 --> 00:39:21.900
Some key takeaways--
to do well here,

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:24.150
everything has to
go right, right?

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:27.090
You have to get the correct
signals for your model.

00:39:27.090 --> 00:39:28.770
You have to train
the model correctly.

00:39:28.770 --> 00:39:31.500
You have to optimize the
model to the correct metrics.

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:33.150
That's a mathematical process.

00:39:33.150 --> 00:39:34.830
You have to optimize for that.

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:36.390
You need good
infrastructure that

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:39.900
can scale to 800 million
users across four or five

00:39:39.900 --> 00:39:42.150
different platforms,
hammering this endpoint,

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:44.100
requesting their predictions.

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:47.230
You need a beautiful and
very usable user experience,

00:39:47.230 --> 00:39:49.620
or otherwise, you're going
to get in the user's way.

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:51.450
It all has to be
super fast, and you

00:39:51.450 --> 00:39:55.050
have to continually improve with
new features and new signals

00:39:55.050 --> 00:39:57.780
and do that in a principled
way using experiments.

00:39:57.780 --> 00:40:01.110
And if you do all of those
things-- and it is an "and"--

00:40:01.110 --> 00:40:04.230
then what we have found
with this dedication,

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:07.560
we can slowly improve the
quality of our predictions

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:10.860
to where we're doing a useful
thing and it's no longer a toy.

00:40:10.860 --> 00:40:12.720
And that is what we've
launched here today.

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:15.330
And we think it's quite magical,
an intelligence feature that

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:19.240
delivers real value to the
user and to the business.

00:40:19.240 --> 00:40:21.870
And then finally, I wanted to
leave us with some key ideas

00:40:21.870 --> 00:40:22.740
here.

00:40:22.740 --> 00:40:25.200
Intelligence features like
Quick Access add value

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:26.010
to the business.

00:40:26.010 --> 00:40:28.290
They enable users to
make better decisions.

00:40:28.290 --> 00:40:32.137
And we've seen that
it saves them time.

00:40:32.137 --> 00:40:34.470
We've seen that I like to say
that intelligence is hard,

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:37.260
and yet, it can be done with
a very rigorous approach.

00:40:37.260 --> 00:40:39.690
You need a lot of
infrastructure and expertise,

00:40:39.690 --> 00:40:43.530
and also a culture around
leadership and product vision,

00:40:43.530 --> 00:40:45.960
and a dedication to
launching and iterating

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:50.160
continuous improvement, the
experiment framework and rigor.

00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:52.710
We've seen that a
compelling UX is critical.

00:40:52.710 --> 00:40:54.730
I can dream up a lot
of very fancy things

00:40:54.730 --> 00:40:57.360
that a neural network can
predict and have no idea how

00:40:57.360 --> 00:41:00.960
to surface them in
the UI, other things

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:03.512
that we could possibly
predict, and just I

00:41:03.512 --> 00:41:04.720
wouldn't know how to do that.

00:41:04.720 --> 00:41:05.430
Or you could do that.

00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:07.800
It would be modal, and now
you're interrupting the user.

00:41:07.800 --> 00:41:10.110
A compelling UX is
critical regardless

00:41:10.110 --> 00:41:12.540
of how good your
neural network accuracy

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:13.980
or area under the curve is.

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:17.140
These things are
very closely related.

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:19.821
And then finally, Quick Access
is a large-scale infrastructure

00:41:19.821 --> 00:41:20.320
project.

00:41:20.320 --> 00:41:23.130
It's very much about
production engineering.

00:41:23.130 --> 00:41:26.064
It's not just about machine
learning or even TensorFlow

00:41:26.064 --> 00:41:27.480
or the machine
learning tool kits.

00:41:27.480 --> 00:41:28.830
It's all the servers.

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:31.020
And then at last,
it's something that's

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:32.940
truly native to the cloud.

00:41:32.940 --> 00:41:35.710
Having the data in the
cloud is how we can do this.

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:38.190
It's not something that you
can do on the filers and impact

00:41:38.190 --> 00:41:39.625
users in a positive
way like this.

00:41:39.625 --> 00:41:42.000
And one of my favorite things
that I've seen about Google

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:44.520
is we have the resources
and leadership buy-in,

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:47.370
and there are machine learning
experts who come to Google,

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:49.700
and we built out our
cloud infrastructure

00:41:49.700 --> 00:41:52.331
all to the point where at
last, we can deliver something

00:41:52.331 --> 00:41:52.830
like this.

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:54.780
And it is truly exhilarating.

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:57.570
There is much more to come, and
I'm grateful to be a part of it

00:41:57.570 --> 00:41:58.309
here with you.

00:41:58.309 --> 00:42:00.850
All righty, everyone, thank you
very much for your attention.

00:42:00.850 --> 00:42:01.620
I appreciate it.

00:42:01.620 --> 00:42:02.920
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:06.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

